<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cherven Towns</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cherven cities</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cherven Towns were a group of fortified settlements in the borderland between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, in the area of present-day south-eastern Poland and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, named after the stronghold of Cherven. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> records that Vladimir I took them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in 981. They changed hands several times until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> recovered them in 1031, and later formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cherven-towns.jpg" alt="Cherven Towns" width="320" height="594" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lonio17 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grody_czerwienskie.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
